/**
 * @defgroup optimal_deviation_factor_calculator Maximum Pump Efficiency Deviation Factor
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief HI 2020 positive relative deviation converted to an efficiency multiplier.
 * @details
 * How does the maximum achievable estimate relate to the corrected average? The result is a
 * dimensionless multiplier above one, not an efficiency or an additive percentage-point increase.
 *
 * @heading{Result and Supporting Data}
 * Interpolate the positive deviation against raw flow in US gpm, then convert it to a multiplier.
 *
 * @formula{hi2020-deviation;
 * D(Q)=1+\frac{d_{positive}(Q)}{100},\qquad \eta_{max}=\eta_{average}D(Q)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{D; Relative maximum-to-average multiplier; \unitless}
 * @symrow{Q; Liquid flow in the US gallon convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{d_{positive}; PCHIP-interpolated positive relative deviation; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage scale per fractional increase; \percent}
 * @symrow{\eta_{max}; Maximum achievable efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Corrected average efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * | Flow (US gpm) | Positive deviation (%) |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | 35 | 8 |
 * | 100 | 6.5 |
 * | 1000 | 3.7 |
 * | 10000 | 2 |
 * | 100000 | 1 |
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * At 500 US gpm, raw-flow PCHIP gives approximately 4.511% positive deviation. The returned
 * multiplier is approximately 1.04511. An average efficiency of 0.8 would therefore give a maximum
 * estimate of about 0.83609 (83.609%), rather than 84.511%.
 *
 * @heading{Provenance and Limits}
 * Data follow HI 20.3-2020 as supplied in the engineering developer reference revised 2026-09-23.
 * Only the positive-deviation path is implemented. This method does not expose the negative table.
 * Interpolation uses raw flow, not log10(flow), and follows @ref pchip_interpolation.
 * Inclusive endpoints are 35 and 100000 US gpm. Any nonfinite or out-of-range flow returns NaN.
 * The full estimate must also satisfy the selected base-curve and specific-speed ranges.
 * No extrapolation or efficiency clamping is applied. The word maximum denotes an empirical estimate,
 * not a guaranteed physical bound or a measured pump characteristic.
 *
 * @see optimal_deviation_factor::calculate
 */
